Your code is already O(n) — the 6558 ms comes almost entirely from the `print()` calls inside the loop. Console I/O on every iteration is extremely slow on LeetCode. Remove those first.

For a further constant-factor win, replace the set + while-loop shrinking with a dict mapping each character to its last seen index. When you hit a duplicate, jump `l` directly past its previous occurrence instead of deleting characters one at a time:

```python
class Solution(object):
    def lengthOfLongestSubstring(self, s):
        last = {}
        l = max_num = 0
        for r, ch in enumerate(s):
            if ch in last and last[ch] >= l:
                l = last[ch] + 1
            last[ch] = r
            if r - l + 1 > max_num:
                max_num = r - l + 1
        return max_num
```

Notes:

- `last[ch] >= l` ignores stale indices of characters already outside the window.
- `if ... > max_num` is marginally faster than calling `max()`.
- Your `continue` and the `if s[r] not in longest` check are redundant — after the while loop, `s[r]` is guaranteed absent from the set.

Both versions are O(n) time, O(min(n, alphabet)) space; this one is a single pass with no inner loop and typically runs in ~50 ms.
